---
import ContentPage from '../layouts/ContentPage.astro';
import { policyPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, POLICY_UPDATED } from '../lib/site';

const toc = [
  { id: 'disclosure', label: 'The disclosure' },
  { id: 'how-it-works', label: 'How affiliate links work' },
  { id: 'what-it-doesnt-affect', label: 'What it does not affect' },
  { id: 'labelling', label: 'How we label links' },
  { id: 'advertising', label: 'Advertising' },
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why we do it this way' },
];
---

<ContentPage
  title="Affiliate Disclosure"
  metaTitle="Affiliate Disclosure | PawLabs"
  description="PawLabs may earn a commission from qualifying purchases made through links on this site. Here is exactly how that works, and what it does and does not influence."
  canonicalUrl="/affiliate-disclosure"
  lede="How PawLabs makes money, and the firewall between that and what we publish."
  updated={POLICY_UPDATED}
  jsonLd={policyPageJsonLd('Affiliate Disclosure', '/affiliate-disclosure', 'How PawLabs earns affiliate commission and what it does not influence.')}
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="disclosure">The disclosure</h2>
  <div class="legal-callout">
    <p>
      <strong>PawLabs is reader-supported. Some links on this site are affiliate links, which means we
      may earn a commission when you buy something through one — at no additional cost to you.</strong>
      You never pay more for using our link than you would going direct.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    This statement is made in line with the US Federal Trade Commission's Guides Concerning the Use of
    Endorsements and Testimonials in Advertising (16 CFR Part 255), and with the disclosure rules of
    the affiliate programmes we take part in. Equivalent rules apply elsewhere — UK CAP Code guidance
    on affiliate marketing, for instance — and this page is written to satisfy all of them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How affiliate links work</h2>
  <p>
    An affiliate link carries a tag identifying PawLabs as the referrer. When you follow one, the
    retailer sets a cookie on their own site. If you then buy something, the retailer pays us a small
    percentage of the sale.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You pay nothing extra.</strong> The commission comes out of the retailer's margin, not your price.</li>
    <li><strong>We do not see your purchase.</strong> Networks report totals — how many referrals converted, and the commission owed. We are not told who you are or what you bought as an individual.</li>
    <li><strong>You can opt out entirely</strong> by blocking third-party cookies, or by searching for the product yourself instead of using our link. Nothing on this site is gated behind an affiliate link.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    What data those cookies involve is set out in the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-doesnt-affect">What it does not affect</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part that matters, because an affiliate site that lets commissions steer its
    recommendations is worthless to you. Ours cannot, structurally:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Trait scores.</strong> All 14 scores on every breed profile come from published kennel
      club standards and veterinary health data. There is no commercial input to a number on this site,
      and no way to buy a higher one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Breed Match quiz results.</strong> Your match is produced by scoring your answers against
      every published breed, then having an AI pick from the highest-scoring shortlist. No breed is
      weighted up because it is commercially convenient, and no advertiser can buy a place in your
      results.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Which breeds we cover.</strong> We profile breeds because they are recognised breeds, not
      because there is money attached to them. Plenty of profiles here have no commercial upside at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What a profile says.</strong> Every profile names the breed's weaknesses and says who
      should not get it. Nobody pays to have that softened.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not publish sponsored posts dressed up as editorial, we do not accept payment for placement
    in a comparison, and we do not let a brand approve copy before it goes live. If that ever changes,
    this page changes first and the arrangement is labelled on the page it affects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="labelling">How we label links</h2>
  <p>
    Where a page carries affiliate links, it says so at the top of that page — not only here. Outbound
    commercial links carry <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code>, which is the machine-readable version
    of the same disclosure and what Google asks publishers to use.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intent is that you should never be surprised. If you clicked a link and did not realise it was
    commercial, we have done this badly and we would like to know:
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('[Disclosure] ')}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="advertising">Advertising</h2>
  <p>
    We may also show display advertising. Ads are served by third-party ad networks, which may use
    cookies to choose what to show you. Ad networks have no involvement in editorial decisions — no
    advertiser sees a page before it is published, and no advertiser can have one changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can control ad personalisation through
    <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Google's My Ad Center</a>
    and through your browser's cookie settings. See the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for what
    is collected.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why we do it this way</h2>
  <p>
    Someone has to pay for 200-odd researched breed profiles, and the realistic options are a paywall,
    advertising and affiliate commission, or selling reader data. We have picked the middle one and
    ruled out the third — see the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>, where we say plainly that we do
    not sell personal information.
  </p>
  <p>
    That leaves one thing worth protecting: your reason to believe the ratings. A trait score is only
    worth reading if it was not for sale. That is the line, and this page exists so you can hold us to
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Questions about any commercial relationship on this site go to
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('[Disclosure] ')}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    We will answer them.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
